{"kind": "candidate", "term": "preponderance of the evidence", "occurrences": 10, "samples": ["confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions.md:40", "confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions.md:76", "confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md:59", "confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md:115", "legal-system-research-and-reference/Case Index.md:261"], "proposed_route": "glossary", "proposed_target": "Common Legal Terms#preponderance-of-the-evidence", "draft_definition": "The more-likely-than-not standard of proof (greater than 50%); the burden a party usually carries in civil cases and on many suppression-hearing sub-issues. It sits below clear and convincing evidence and far below beyond a reasonable doubt.", "rationale": "Cross-cutting burden term of art; page-less; recurs in confessions/proof-ladder prose. Pure definition, no case. ALT: route:page [[The Proof Ladder]]."}
